1. THE PINS ON THE SUN (nature's numbers): k = G M / (r c^2) at the limb, the deflection 4 k, the Shapiro delay's coefficient 2 G M / c^3
   k at the limb = 2.122e-06; Newton's deflection 2 k = 0.876 arcsec; Einstein's 4 k = 1.751 arcsec (Dyson, Eddington and Davidson 1920: 1.98 +- 0.16 and 1.61 +- 0.40; VLBI: gamma = 0.99992 +- 0.00012, Lambert and Le Poncin-Lafitte 2011)
   the Shapiro delay at Cassini's geometry (Earth at 1 au, Saturn at 8.43 au, the ray at 1.6 solar radii): (2 G M / c^3) ln(4 r_1 r_2 / b^2) = 131 microseconds; Cassini's gamma - 1 = (2.1 +- 2.3) x 10^-5 (Bertotti, Iess and Tortora 2003)
   the second-order redshift: nature's 1 + z = 1 / sqrt(1 - 2 k) = 1 + k + (3 / 2) k^2 + (5 / 2) k^3 ...; the law today 1 + z = 1 + k exactly (the clock at 1 / (1 + k), 5.2)

2. THE RULE'S INTEGERS ON SERIES K'S WORLDS: the rows' pair is the clock's suspension pair [n_s, d_s]; series K registers suspension 0 (the rows blind, 0.000 kept); the pin world at [1, 256]
   [n_s, d_s] = [1, 256]: k(b) = (n_s / d_s) x (the age moment at b); the wall of the flight 2 T_D (d_s + 2 n_s A) / d_s; the deflection 4 k(b); the delay (2 k(b) b / c) ln(4 r_1 r_2 / b^2)
   world | M | b | k(b) | the deflection 4 k(b), radians | the centroid's shift on the screen, pixels (26 Links past the mass) | the delay, intervals | the mean age (89.40 registered) | the lamp's own clock at r = 26, its rate | the phase rate read (8 registered)
   mass  | 2^12 | 6 | 0.0445 | 0.1781 | -4.63 | 3.97 | 93.37 | 0.9898 | 7.919
   heavy | 2^13 | 6 | 0.0887 | 0.3547 | -9.22 | 7.90 | 97.30 | 0.9799 | 7.840
   near  | 2^12 | 3 | 0.0887 | 0.3547 | -9.22 | 5.22 | 94.62 | 0.9899 | 7.919
   the bracket of series K is 0.5 pixel and 1 interval: every shift and every delay above is outside it, toward the mass and later, and the two grow together with k(b) as one number
   the factor 2 is the rule's one new integer: at [n_s, d_s] with the factor 1 (Newton's) the shifts and the delays are half of the above; Cassini's bound reads the factor as 2 to 2 x 10^-5

3. THE TURN ON THE FAN: the step at the light's directions (2.4 degrees between (1, 0, 0) and (24, +-1, 0)), the mean turn as a fraction of one step, the rows that turn one step under the wheel's dither
   mass : the mean turn 0.1781 rad = 4.28 steps of 0.0416 rad; of 122 rays about 34 turn one more step than the rest; the centroid's mean exact, the per-ray turn whole
   heavy: the mean turn 0.3547 rad = 8.52 steps of 0.0416 rad; of 122 rays about 63 turn one more step than the rest; the centroid's mean exact, the per-ray turn whole
   near : the mean turn 0.3547 rad = 8.52 steps of 0.0416 rad; of 122 rays about 63 turn one more step than the rest; the centroid's mean exact, the per-ray turn whole

4. THE SECOND-ORDER REDSHIFT: not the rows' rule (a static crowd stretches no stream); the clock's owed rate with one bilinear term, w = k' + (3 / 2) k'^2 in integers (2 k n d + 3 k^2 n^2) / (2 d^2)
   k' = 6.25e-02: 1 + z today 1.0625000, with the quadratic term 1.0683594, nature's 1.0690450; the residual -6.9e-04 (the third order, (5 / 2) k'^3 = 6.1e-04)
   k' = 1.56e-02: 1 + z today 1.0156250, with the quadratic term 1.0159912, nature's 1.0160010; the residual -9.8e-06 (the third order, (5 / 2) k'^3 = 9.5e-06)
   k' = 1.00e-03: 1 + z today 1.0010000, with the quadratic term 1.0010015, nature's 1.0010015; the residual -2.5e-09 (the third order, (5 / 2) k'^3 = 2.5e-09)
   k' = 2.10e-06: 1 + z today 1.0000021, with the quadratic term 1.0000021, nature's 1.0000021; the residual -2.2e-16 (the third order, (5 / 2) k'^3 = 2.3e-17)
   the pin: a lamp at k' = 1 / 16 read by a clock at k' = 0: 1 + z = 1.0625 today, 1.0684 with the term, nature's 1.0690; the rate at most bilinear in the state (k x k), no root

5. THE MAP AT ONE NODE, THE COST AGAINST K (the operations per row per interval; 13.1's counts for the walk 16 and the reading 26 per row read)
   the Node's moments of the other numbers' rows (the presence, the flow V, the age moment A)                               | 26 per row read, once per Node per interval; today only at Nodes with a body, under the rule at every Node with a row
   the wall of the flight: 2 T_D (d_s + 2 n_s A), one multiply and one add                                                  | 2
   the walk against the wall (the carry, the deficits)                                                                      | 16 (today's)
   the turn accumulators per transverse axis: w_a += 2 n_s T_D V_a, two multiplies and two adds                             | 4
   the step of the direction label when |w_a| crosses the wall d_s Q^2 x step (a comparison per axis, the fan's neighbour by the table) | 2 + the table's lookup
   the wheel's dither of the accumulator at birth: one multiply and one division                                            | 2, once per row
   the store per row: two integers (the turn accumulators); nothing at the Node (the moments are readings of the rows present, recomputed per interval)

6. THE VERDICT: REACHABLE IN FORM for the bending and the delay (one identity, optical-v1: the flight's wall and the turn read the crowd's age moment and flow at the row's Node with the clock's pair and the factor 2), the pin series K at [1, 256]; the second-order redshift a companion term on the clock's rate, not the rows'; the run waits on the owner's go and the physics-rule review

7. SNELL'S LAW AT A BOUNDARY OF TWO CROWDS: n = 1 + 2 k_a the pace ratio; the rule's turn across the boundary is Delta theta = -(n_2 - n_1) tan theta_1 (Fermat's ray equation at n near 1); Snell's n_1 sin theta_1 = n_2 sin theta_2
   the pin before the numbers: the rule gives Snell to first order in n_2 - n_1 = 2 (k_2 - k_1), the residual of order (n_2 - n_1)^2; the sign toward the denser crowd
   k_2 = 1/256 (n_2 = 1.00781), theta_1 = 30 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 29.744 deg, the rule's 29.742 deg, the difference -0.002 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 6.1e-05)
   k_2 = 1/256 (n_2 = 1.00781), theta_1 = 60 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 59.239 deg, the rule's 59.225 deg, the difference -0.015 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 6.1e-05)
   k_2 = 1/64 (n_2 = 1.03125), theta_1 = 30 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 29.003 deg, the rule's 28.966 deg, the difference -0.036 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 9.8e-04)
   k_2 = 1/64 (n_2 = 1.03125), theta_1 = 60 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 57.117 deg, the rule's 56.899 deg, the difference -0.218 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 9.8e-04)
   k_2 = 1/16 (n_2 = 1.12500), theta_1 = 30 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 26.388 deg, the rule's 25.865 deg, the difference -0.523 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 1.6e-02)
   k_2 = 1/16 (n_2 = 1.12500), theta_1 = 60 deg: Snell's theta_2 = 50.336 deg, the rule's 47.595 deg, the difference -2.741 deg (order (n_2 - 1)^2 = 1.6e-02)
   on the fan: the turn is whole steps (2.4 degrees at the light's directions of series K), the wheel's dither making the mean exact over a beam; a pin reads a beam's centroid, not one row
   Fermat's least time: the rule is the ray equation d theta / dl = grad_perp(2 k_a), Fermat's Euler-Lagrange form at n near 1; the fan's choice of direction (the Huygens fan of TWO_SLITS section 7) gives the least-time path as the click's stationary phase only if the row's phase advances per interval (a clock, the slits' declaration), not per Link
